@LupusTheCanine  Is the main rotor maintaining RPM?  Is this a clockwise rotating rotor when viewed from above?  if the main rotor RPM is not governed then as the battery loses voltage, RPM will reduce as well.  For a clockwise rotating rotor, tail rotor is thrusting to the left thus causing a right yaw moment.  For a rotor losing RPM the trim would travel to the left (negative in this case) because less right yaw moment would be needed as the torque decreases from the main rotor decreasing RPM.  

If this was a tail rotor thrust linearization issue where battery compensation is not working, then as the battery voltage decreased then more right yaw input would be needed to overcome the same torque of a rotor at constant rotor speed.  

