@peterbarker Thanks for pointing out the checked registers—this is definitely the right direction for sensor attestation. As mentioned in my previous comment and noted by @LupusTheCanine, attestation by itself doesn’t remove the root issue, but it does enable us to detect it.

While reviewing the BMI055 driver, I noticed that register checks only occur if new data arrives (if `read_registers` calls fail, the code returns without calling the `check_next_register`). One way to improve this would be to run those checks asynchronously, rather than tying them to data availability. In the current setting, upon sensor suspension, no data would be available, preventing the check from taking place (this depends on the sensor behavior; we found some sensors keep reporting default values upon suspension). Making these checks asynchronous and setting the checked register a priori would enable reconfiguration detection. 

